{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D51-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"880082f644a2b5dacf2c3c5ff042cab843ab0cc33b266b71474e1d5507ddc1ec\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 demands an apology for interrupting the chair; A3 rejects compensation directed at trousers.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's trousers produce A3's office distinction; B4's 'you' produces A5's correction to 'Chair.'\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 addresses the visible accident and B4 then concedes the interruption, allowing A to narrow the demand.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 requires the corrected apology to acknowledge rank after B4 already apologizes personally.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a chaired gathering, interruption and spilled tea; B2 establishes whose trousers were hit.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'trousers hold no office' expresses the exact distinction economically; A5 adds one further correction.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 92\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Your apology should mention interrupting the chair, not just spilling the tea.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm sorry about your trousers.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The trousers hold no office.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm sorry I interrupted you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"'Chair.' Let's repair the title while we're here.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A makes B repair disrespect to an office through an apology that explicitly names that office.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D57 also demands recognition through a title, but does not require an apology for disrespect.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 specifies the interruption as the offense, and A5 edits B4's apology rather than requesting an ordinary introduction.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a corrects an apology face to face; alternative b seeks a written acknowledgment after humiliation before trainees.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged: both replies cause narrower demands for recognition of the chair's position. Internal rereading supports the original ratings and all spoken-turn gates.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 approaches D57's title mechanism, although its placement inside the apology keeps D51 clear.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D51-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"a59a205aab91d0373411e6806bc30b1d6d7e2aad5faeafae6662af3c5d1fa09e\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 rejects regret about the failed plan; A5 requires the apology email to display A's position.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's misplaced apology; A5 uses B4's offered email, though its recipients remain unstated.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 apologizes for the result and B4 offers a practical repair; A changes what that repair must recognize.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4's email offer lets A5 turn an interpersonal apology into a visible declaration of position.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 names the trainees and the insult, but A5 leaves the actual position unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's private/public distinction leads economically to A5's status-conscious subject line.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 86\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You called my plan silly in front of the trainees.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm sorry it didn't work.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The plan failed privately. You disrespected me publicly.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Would you like an email?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"With my position in the subject line.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A redirects an apology about a failed result toward acknowledgment of disrespect to A's professional position.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Personal wounded pride would seek acknowledgment of hurt without requiring recognition of professional standing.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 distinguishes failure from disrespect, and A5 expressly requires A's position in the apology's subject line.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b uses a cooperative offer to obtain documentary recognition; alternative a insists on precise spoken wording.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged: the email solution becomes the next expression of the drive. Internal rereading confirms the original ratings and causal links.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The position and email audience are implicit, so the public repair is less fully specified than the offense.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D52-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"806d35f82c57b9f4d7b5c7ad7efc4d7a67dc784c1a0c0f6644a9c83d7f3c4771\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 explicitly needs endorsement to provide protection at the staff meeting; A5 seeks relevant witnesses.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's private assent causes A3's publicity requirement; B4's email offer becomes A5's expanded copy list.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 considers the support already given and B4 proposes a less theatrical channel than A1's microphone.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 turns B4's private email offer into circulation to every potential critic.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a rota endorsement and microphone; A3 supplies the impending staff meeting.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's repeated 'Quietly' states the practical defect; A5's copy list gives the email a defensive purpose.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Would you endorse the new rota into this microphone?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I already said it was fine.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Quietly. Quietly can't protect me at the staff meeting.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Can I email you?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Copy everyone who might blame me.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A converts private agreement into visible backing that can shield the rota decision from challenge.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D55 recruits a broader coalition; D52 can use a single person's visible endorsement as protection.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"B2 already agrees, while A3 and A5 pursue exposure of that agreement to potential critics.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a negotiates the communication channel with a willing supporter; alternative b confronts a supporter who wants anonymity.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged: B's helpful email offer directly enables the final protective demand. Internal rereading confirms the original ratings.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The microphone is theatrical, and B's particular influence over the staff meeting is left implicit.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D52-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"7c79063738776f6feee5204592d103477f9e047d990ef10ad03efde2d0e30459\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 requests signed support; A3 explains its value when objections arrive; A5 prevents anonymous endorsement.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2 withholds a name, prompting A3's accountability argument; B4's initials produce A5's printed key.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 likes the extension but resists exposure; B4 bargains for reduced visibility.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 accepts the initials only by adding a public decoding device that defeats B4's attempted retreat.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 provides a document and extension proposal, although the kind of extension is unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's support having 'no address' motivates the concise printed-key solution in A5.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Sign here to say you support the extension.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I like it, but I'd rather not put my name down.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then your support has no address when objections arrive.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Could I use initials?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Only if we print the key beside them.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A demands an identifiable endorsement so B's support remains usable when the proposal faces objections.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A loyalty demand would prioritize choosing A; this exchange prioritizes making already offered support attributable.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"B2 already likes the proposal, and A5 specifically solves the anonymity of B4's initials.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b makes identity the bargaining point; alternative a makes distribution to possible critics the bargaining point.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged: the proposed initials materially cause the final requirement. Internal rereading confirms the original ratings and acceptance.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The extension and the signatories' relationship could be more concrete, but the document negotiation is immediately playable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D53-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"c7382d4883a6aed89588b843800f944f006bb3139f12d7102d814458aced36a7\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 assigns the sink through junior rank and A5 claims senior exemption, but A3 mainly repackages work as training.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 echoes B2's coordination achievement; A5 answers B4's accusation without changing the assignment.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 asserts earned competence and B4 challenges A's motive, establishing a credible junior's grievance.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 repeats the senior/junior justification already present in A1 without adding responsibility or pressure.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 names the blocked sink and junior coordinator; B2 supplies yesterday's event.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'drainage leadership' grows from B2, but A5 is a familiar delegation maxim.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"As junior coordinator, you're responsible for the blocked sink.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I ran the whole event yesterday. You said I'd earned a promotion.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"To senior coordinator. Still below me, but now you can supervise the plumber.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"He can only come after everyone leaves.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then you'll lock up too. I can't give building security to a junior.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A places B below A in the hierarchy to make B accept unwanted work, using limited promotion to reinforce the assignment.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic task avoidance can pass work along without making lower rank the reason the other person must accept it.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 names junior rank and A5 claims senior exemption; revised A3 preserves B's subordinate position even while promoting B.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a exploits a promised promotion and an inconvenient plumber visit; alternative b uses reporting procedure to attach complaints to a deputy.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Original A5 largely restated A1. Revised setting retains the event sink and adds a plumber. Internal second pass: fidelity 5 (A3 keeps B below A), logic 5 (B2 causes promotion; B4 causes lockup), relationship 4 (B seeks advancement), heightening 4 (A5 adds late responsibility), specificity 4 (event and plumber), brevity/wit 4 (promotion earns more work): 90. Five ABABA turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A's improvised promotion needs sincere managerial delivery; played knowingly, it could become a transparent gag.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D53-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"adc848b2e573ce5c0291cb1f8d2504965f33f93461269546384b9d1bb1081327\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 sends notes down the chain and A5 reserves senior work, but acceptance of actual complaint responsibility is implicit.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 reframes B2's complaints as education; A5 responds directly to B4's question about compliments.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 identifies the undesirable contents and B4 exposes the unequal allocation of praise.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 explains the existing split instead of increasing B's obligation after B4 challenges it.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes inspection notes, and B2 and B4 give actors contrasting piles of feedback.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's senior signature is concise status logic, though it supplies little new pressure.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You're my deputy. You'll answer the inspector's complaints.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Most of them concern decisions you made.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Exactly. You handle the details below decision-making level.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The inspector wants whoever signs the replies at tomorrow's hearing.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Sign as deputy, then. You can represent me without giving the impression we're equals.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A uses B's subordinate rank to assign unpleasant accountability while preserving A's superior standing.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Blame shifting alone would explain evasion but would not require establishing B as lower in the hierarchy.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 invokes the chain and A5 senior privilege; revised A3 and A5 make subordinate status the rationale for handling the consequences.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b transfers formal accountability through a deputy's signature; alternative a uses advancement to impose practical after-hours work.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Original A5 defended a division of paperwork without developing the burden. Revised inspection setting adds a hearing. Internal second pass: fidelity 5 (A1/A3 subordinate assignment), logic 5 (B2 decisions cause A3's distinction; B4 signing causes A5), relationship 4 (B exposes A's responsibility), heightening 4 (A5 assigns attendance), specificity 4 (inspector and hearing), brevity/wit 4 (representation preserves inequality): 90. Spoken ABABA and word limit pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The invented inspection procedure is illustrative scene logic; the hearing's precise institutional context remains open.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D54-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"c4aad87a542339838e95fbd307cffcbe8a414665dfcb5ad91a8fb1f50972ec8c\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 searches for cancellation power despite approval; A3 tests who could override the committee.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's unanimous signature prompts A3's override question; B4's treasurer revelation determines A5's destination.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 offers reassurance and B4 provides information, but B has little stake beyond answering A.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 locates the named treasurer without testing the newly revealed control over the deposit.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies the trip, B2 the committee approval, and B4 the deposit.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's phone call tearing up unanimity crisply contrasts official approval with practical control.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 84\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before I book the trip, who can still cancel it?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The committee approved it. Please book before we lose the coach.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Whose objection would stop you paying the deposit?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The treasurer's. But she's away, and her deputy has the bank login.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Can the deputy release it alone, or does she still need the treasurer's permission?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A probes successive layers of apparent approval to identify who actually controls the outcome.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Chain of Command follows an established hierarchy; this drive investigates whether formal approval corresponds to actual power.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 questions whether a phone call overrides the signed approval; revised A5 tests whether bank access entails payment authority.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a traces booking and payment power under time pressure; alternative b traces who can reverse a repair-funding freeze.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Original A5 stopped at locating the treasurer. Revised trip setting adds booking urgency and delegated bank access. Internal second pass: fidelity 5 (A3/A5 test control), logic 5 (B2 payment urgency informs A3; B4 access informs A5), relationship 4 (B wants the coach booked), heightening 4 (A5 separates access from permission), specificity 4 (deposit and deputy), brevity/wit 4 (practical authority puzzle): 90. ABABA and word limit pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The revised scene favors clear investigation over a strong laugh; its final question deliberately leaves the investigation open.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D54-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"3940aa6aced06363d8e59197f0157dc92e51e5bfa451a165dedd36598e1f7e67\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 asks which signature releases money, and A3 tests which can override the others.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's equal-importance answer causes A3's override test; B4 identifies finance, which A5 pursues.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 defends the signatures' importance, but B4 mainly supplies the department needed for A5.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 announces a visit to finance instead of further distinguishing who there can approve or reverse the freeze.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes repair money and three signatures; A5 specifies the broken pipe.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's broken pipe gives the bureaucratic search a physical comic image tied to the repair.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 84\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The pipe's still leaking. Which signature releases the repair money?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"All three. I've spent all morning getting them.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Could anyone still freeze it after your morning's work?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Finance could. Though they unfroze the roof payment when the caretaker called.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"What did the caretaker have: permission to overrule finance, or someone else's direct number?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A tests formal authorization against evidence of who can block or reverse a funding decision.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple escalation seeks someone more senior; this investigation also considers an informal contact as the source of effective power.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 tests override power; revised A5 asks whether the caretaker personally holds authority or merely reaches its holder.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b uses a previous reversal to investigate informal influence; alternative a tests a deputy's practical access against permission.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Original A5 ended the inquiry at a department name. Revised repair setting adds B's effort and a roof-payment precedent. Internal second pass: fidelity 5 (A3/A5 probe control), logic 5 (B2 effort informs A3; B4 precedent causes A5), relationship 4 (B defends completed work), heightening 4 (A5 tests an unexpected authority route), specificity 4 (leak and roof payment), brevity/wit 4 (caretaker complicates signatures): 90. ABABA and word limit pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 offers two possible explanations without settling them; continued play must remain curious rather than treating the caretaker as automatically powerful.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D55-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"7e07eb644f8eba864370904428b677a0ed94021688bcd6c795bb69ee9c25d5d5\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 recruits visible company for broad support; A3 seeks appearance despite B2's undecided position.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 incorporates B2's indecision into seating; A5 explicitly confirms B4's interpretation of the seating.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 preserves neutrality and B4 names the misleading implication of joining A.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 acknowledges the plan already stated in A1 rather than developing recruitment after B4's objection.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a shed vote and opposing seating; the proposed shed position itself is unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'broadly undecided' adapts B2 neatly, but A5 mostly explains the existing joke.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 81\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Sit with me for the allotment shed vote. It shouldn't look like just my project.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I only support it if everyone gets a key.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Shared keys. Agreed. Will you join me now?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The new plot holders need to hear that.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Bring them over. We can present it together as the shared-access proposal.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A recruits another participant and incorporates their condition so the proposal gains broader, more legitimate ownership.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D52 secures visible endorsement; coalition building here expands participation and lets recruited people shape the position.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 wants broad support; revised A3 accepts shared keys and A5 invites the new plot holders into a joint proposal.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a builds support through a substantive concession; alternative b seeks names from another group to escape the appearance of a personal grievance.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Original A5 explained the seating tactic without increasing recruitment. Revised setting specifies an allotment and shared access. Internal second pass: fidelity 5 (A1/A5 broaden ownership), logic 5 (B2 causes shared keys; B4 causes outreach), relationship 4 (B wants universal access), heightening 4 (A5 recruits a group), specificity 4 (shed and plot holders), brevity/wit 4 (A repackages the concession as collective legitimacy): 90. ABABA and word limit pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The revised exchange is cooperative and lightly comic; the recruitment mechanism is stronger than the punchline.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D55-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"0b60ec9a9904a38c575bf04b34dfc85ffc9a0c258786a53f9ff1e38866afcac0\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 seeks provisional solidarity and A5 wants names to confer legitimacy, but no actual position is stated.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2's unread petition causes A3's pencil compromise; A5 deliberately evades B4's request for its subject.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 reasonably seek information, but A never engages any substantive interest B might hold.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 expands the desired name count while leaving the initial recruitment request unresolved and abstract.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a petition but neither A1 nor A5 reveals its subject or the participants' relationship.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's pencil solidarity and A5's circular legitimacy argument are concise expressions of recruitment without scrutiny.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 71\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Will you join my request to keep the pottery room open late? It looks like a personal favour with one name.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I leave at five.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Your name would show it's bigger than the evening class.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The morning class wants weekend access.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Add that. Could you bring two of them? We'd be several classes asking for better access.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A recruits people beyond the immediate beneficiary group so a request appears collective and legitimate.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Collecting a signature for endorsement need not broaden the represented constituency or incorporate another group's demand.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 treats accumulated names as legitimacy; revised A3 targets another class and A5 combines its interest with A's request.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b pursues cross-class representation through a combined access request; alternative a directly concedes shared keys to a prospective ally.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original withheld the petition's subject throughout. New illustrative setting: pottery-room access. Internal second pass: fidelity 5 (A3/A5 broaden constituency), logic 5 (B2's hours inform A3; B4's weekend interest informs A5), relationship 4 (B supplies another group's need), heightening 4 (A5 expands platform and recruitment), specificity 4 (class schedules), brevity/wit 4 (a personal favour becomes a collective access demand): 90. ABABA and 75-word limit pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A still values B partly as a representative name; sincere interest in the combined request should coexist with that instrumental motive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D56-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"9037f333c6a828d30079ca830cfa6800519ccfaf9fb86024d471b9c135fa89bc\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 sends B to question the director; A3 and A5 explain A's avoidance through disciplinary and employment risk.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2 asks why A cannot ask, producing A3's history; B4 requests proximity, producing A5's protective positioning.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 challenges the burden and B4 seeks solidarity rather than accepting unsupported exposure.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 converts B4's request for visible solidarity into physical cover while continuing to offer support.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 names the director and overtime rule; A3 supplies the particular reason A fears speaking.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'Just behind' carries the avoidance in two words before the employment rationale completes it.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Ask the director why the overtime rule changed. I'll support you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Why don't you ask?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Your fresh perspective won't carry my disciplinary history.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Will you stand beside me?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Just behind. Where supportive people remain employable.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A persuades B to challenge authority first while recasting A's own reduced exposure as supportive participation.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Start the Hard Part delegates emotional difficulty; D56 specifically delegates the initial challenge to a rule or authority.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 identifies the director's overtime rule, while A3 and A5 make disciplinary and employment consequences the avoided risk.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a bargains over physical solidarity under a stated disciplinary risk; alternative b substitutes preparatory labor for a public signature.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged: both B turns demand accountability and each causes a specific evasive support tactic. Internal rereading confirms the original ratings.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The final employment phrase is polished; A should speak from practical fear rather than wink at the audience.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D56-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"41fdef24d22248a9146f4b2dd6ab651cb060114a722c616222071f837a045e87\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n  