y for an explicit renewed allegiance demand.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's preferred parts to demand a name; A5 answers B4's why-question with a weak practical rationale.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants a mixed proposal and B4 questions the need for factional alignment.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 restates the binary through seating without making B's choice more consequential or specific.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 names rival Morgan and competing proposals, but their subject remains unspecified through A5.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's name above the favorite parts is concise; A5's table image feels more manufactured than necessary.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"For the workshop move, are you backing my proposal or Morgan's?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Your location, Morgan's budget.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Fine. Whose proposal will you call that in the meeting?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll call it a compromise.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"And when Morgan and I both claim you supported us, which one will you correct?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A forces B to declare support for a particular claimant despite B's attempt to combine the merits of both proposals.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Coalition building can accept a shared platform; this drive continues demanding a side even after substantive compromise is available.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 asks whose name heads the preferred parts; revised A5 requires B to correct one of two competing claims of support.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a blocks neutrality through a future public attribution dilemma; alternative b makes a ribbon bargain require an explicit choice between candidates in a club election.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Original A5 used a flimsy seating rationale. Revised setting specifies a workshop move. Internal second pass: fidelity 5 (A3/A5 demand a claimant), logic 5 (B2's mix informs A3; B4's compromise causes A5's attribution test), relationship 4 (B seeks a combined solution), heightening 4 (A5 demands public correction), specificity 4 (location and budget), brevity/wit 4 (compromise creates rival claims): 90. ABABA and word limit pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 anticipates a future dispute rather than creating an immediate deadline, but it gives B a concrete choice to answer.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D58-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"d058cb052aab617283dfd1ca9420372a4fb013ee0795dd999b1409d32fbaae03\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 solicits campaign allegiance and A3 treats neutrality as suspect, but A5 mainly repeats recruitment to A's side.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2's neutrality prompts A3's attribution question; B4's 'Neither' enables A5's vacancy claim.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 states a clear wish to remain neutral and B4 refuses A3's implied hidden allegiance.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 calls neutrality a vacancy but adds no new choice, cost or pressure beyond A1's invitation.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a club election and campaign ribbon, although no rival is identified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's vacancy metaphor follows B4's unclaimed status, but softens the demanded choice into recruitment.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"s